<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">James II of Scotland</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1430">1430</a>–<a href="/years/1460">1460</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-stuart">House of Stuart</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">James II was King of Scots from 1437 to 1460, known for the red birthmark on his face. After a minority dominated by noble factions, he broke the power of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clan-douglas">Black Douglases</a>, killing the eighth earl in 1452. An enthusiast for artillery, he was killed in 1460 when one of his own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a> burst during the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roxburgh-castle">Roxburgh Castle</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-ii-of-scotland.jpg" alt="James II of Scotland" width="320" height="441" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author to Georg (Jörg) von Ehingen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:James_II_of_Scotland_(by_von_Ehingen).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>James was born at Holyrood Palace on 16 October 1430, the younger twin son of James I and Joan Beaufort. His brother Alexander died within months, leaving him the only surviving son. The Auchinleck chronicle called his birthmark, which covered one side of his face, the fyre mark in his face. He was crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holyrood-abbey">Holyrood Abbey</a> on 26 March 1437, aged six. The regency was marked by disorder, famine and plague. On 24 November 1440 the regents Crichton and Avandale invited the young William, 6th Earl of Douglas, to Edinburgh Castle, where he was beheaded in the event known as the Black Dinner.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The Douglas family dominated Scottish politics in the 1440s under William, 8th Earl of Douglas, who in 1443 used the twelve-year-old king's banner to take a Crichton tower. James began his personal rule in 1449, and married Mary of Guelders at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holyrood-abbey">Holyrood Abbey</a> on 3 July 1449. In September 1449 he attacked the Livingston family, and the comptroller Robert Livingston was executed. In 1451 John, Earl of Ross, attacked royal castles, and Douglas, allied with Ross and the Earl of Crawford, refused to support the king.</p><p>On 22 February 1452 the king and Douglas dined at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stirling">Stirling</a>. James asked him to dissolve the alliance, Douglas refused, and James stabbed him twice before his attendants joined in. The killing of a man under royal protection damaged James's standing.</p><p>The new earl, James, 9th Earl of Douglas, burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stirling">Stirling</a> in March 1452. A parliament cleared the king of the murder. In August 1455 parliament disinherited the Douglases for treason, and James distributed their lands, creating new earldoms after 1457. He attacked English Berwick in 1455, exploiting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-roses">Wars of the Roses</a>, and renounced the truce with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in May 1456.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>James was killed at Roxburgh on 3 August 1460 when a bombard near him exploded, probably during a salute for the queen's arrival at the siege. The historian John Major blamed him for being over-curious in the matter of engines of war. The castle fell on 8 August, and James III was crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kelso-abbey">Kelso Abbey</a> on 10 August. James II was buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holyrood-abbey">Holyrood Abbey</a>. His widow Mary of Guelders became the leading figure in Scottish politics and was formally appointed regent in 1461. James has been described as a largely successful king who strengthened the Scottish monarchy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “James II of Scotland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>James II of Scotland</b>, an enthusiast for artillery, was killed in 1460 when one of his own cannons burst during the siege of Roxburgh Castle.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holyrood-abbey">Holyrood Abbey</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Roxburgh, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.95,-3.17&amp;year=1449" aria-label="Holyrood Abbey on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4481 258 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of James II of Scotland"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4931 528h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4931 528h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.95,-3.17&amp;year=1449">What was here in 1449? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
